        <!-- Closing CTA -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            <strong class="text-white">Hold the rails to get on and off, through speed changes, when
            anything unexpected happens, and throughout if you need support.</strong> Safety comes
            first, and nobody who needs the rails should feel they are exercising wrongly by using
            them.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            If you can walk safely hands-free, though, habitual gripping takes work from your legs,
            bends your posture and leaves the console overstating what you did. Lower the speed or
            incline until you can let go, then build back up. For how speed, incline and weight turn
            into a calorie estimate, see our reference on
            <a href="/treadmill-calories-burned/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">treadmill calories burned</a>.
          </p>
        </div>
